<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>A worked example: the default numbers</h2>
    <p>$10,000 invested at 7% a year, compounded monthly, with $200 added each month for 20 years, grows to $144,572.72. You put in $58,000 ($10,000 up front plus $48,000 in deposits); the other $86,572.72 is growth. By the end, growth is more than half the balance, and it keeps accelerating: the last 5 years add more than the first 10.</p>

    <h2>Time matters far more than compounding frequency</h2>
    <p>Here is $10,000 at 7% for 20 years with no deposits, compounded at different frequencies:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Compounding</th><th class="num">Balance after 20 years</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Annually</td><td class="num">$38,696.84</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Quarterly</td><td class="num">$40,063.92</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Monthly</td><td class="num">$40,387.39</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Daily</td><td class="num">$40,546.56</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Going from monthly to daily compounding adds $159 over 20 years. Adding time does far more: the same $200 a month at 7% grows to $104,185 over 20 years, but $243,994 over 30 years. The extra 10 years add $24,000 in deposits and nearly $140,000 in balance. Starting early is the lever that matters most.</p>

    <h2>The rule of 72</h2>
    <p>Divide 72 by the annual return to estimate how many years money takes to double. At 7%, that is about 10.3 years (the exact figure is 10.24). At 4%, about 18 years; at 10%, about 7. It works best for rates between about 4% and 12%, and it is a quick way to sanity-check any projection.</p>

    <h2>Rate vs. APY</h2>
    <p>Enter the stated annual (nominal) rate and choose the compounding frequency; the calculator does the compounding. Savings accounts usually advertise APY, which already includes compounding: 5% compounded monthly is an APY of 5.12%. If you enter an APY and also select monthly compounding, the result will be slightly too high. To use an APY directly, enter it with annual compounding.</p>

    <h2>What the projection leaves out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Inflation.</strong> The result is in future dollars. At 3% inflation, the default example's $144,573 in 20 years buys about what $80,000 buys today. To project in today's dollars, enter the return minus inflation (about 3.9% here) as the rate; that version assumes your deposits rise with inflation too.</li>
      <li><strong>Volatility.</strong> Investments don't return a steady 7% each year. The order of good and bad years matters, especially when you are adding or withdrawing money. Treat the result as an average-case illustration.</li>
      <li><strong>Fees and taxes.</strong> A 1% annual fee is a full point off the rate. At 6% instead of 7%, the default example ends at $125,510, about $19,000 less.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Compound interest questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How much will $10,000 grow in 20 years at 7%?</summary><p>About $40,387 with monthly compounding and no additions. With $200 added every month, it grows to about $144,573.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How long does it take money to double?</summary><p>Divide 72 by the annual rate. At 7%, money doubles in about 10 years; at 10%, about 7 years.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does daily compounding make a big difference?</summary><p>Very little. $10,000 at 7% for 20 years is $40,387 compounded monthly and $40,547 compounded daily. The rate and the number of years matter far more.</p></details>
